## Resources & AI Use Description

### References & Documentation
* **42 Push_swap Subject Documentation**: System limitations, instruction constraints, and boundary validation rules.
* **Divide and Conquer Paradigms**: Structural design guidelines for median-driven quicksort implementations.
* **Asymptotic Notation & Time Complexity Studies**: Theoretical foundations explaining the behavior of \(O(n^2)\), \(O(n\sqrt{n})\), and \(O(n \log n)\) tracking curves.

### Description of AI Assistance
Artificial Intelligence was actively leveraged as a collaborative peer during the code validation, architectural audit, and documentation engineering phases of this project.

* **Code Verification & Architecture Auditing:** AI was used to trace complex nested memory conditions within the recursive `quick_sort_b` loop to confirm that rollback structures (`rrb`) accurately protected lower pre-sorted blocks from corruption.
* **Educational Refactoring:** AI assisted in analyzing the mathematical constraints of the square-root chunk factor adjustments, helping to map out why scaling chunks dynamically via \(\sqrt{n}\) structurally optimizes the rotation costs compared to static indexing.
* **Defense Preparation & Explanations:** AI was utilized to draft universal, clear analogies to translate raw pointer logic into high-level conceptual summaries for peer-evaluators.

